(a) Decisions on motions and objections. All motions and objections that are brought before City Council may be delegated to City Council’s legal and/or judicial advisor for a recommendation. City Council will not be bound by the recommendation of the advisor but may take it into account when rendering a decision.

(b) Decision by City Council. Two-thirds of City Council Members serving must be present in order for the body to conduct a forfeiture proceeding, or any portion of a forfeiture proceeding. Further, two-thirds of City Council Members serving must be present to conduct a vote, preliminary or final, on the issue of formally initiating the process of forfeiture of elective office, through a final forfeiture decision under Section 2-107 of the City Charter. A final decision of City Council to remove an elective city officer from office shall require a vote of two-thirds of Members serving.

(c) Methodology for decision-making; burden of proof. In any action before City Council to initiate forfeiture, the petitioning party must prove his, her, or its case by clear and convincing evidence.

(1) If the petitioner proves by clear and convincing evidence that the elected official against whom forfeiture is sought is guilty of any of the following, forfeiture of office is mandatory:$^{h}$

a. Lacks at any time any qualifications required by law or by Section 2-101 of the City Charter;

b. Has pled to, or been convicted of, a felony while holding the office;

c. Has engaged in official misconduct;

d. Is guilty of willful or gross neglect of duty;

e. Is guilty of corrupt conduct in office; or

f. Has committed any other misfeasance or malfeasance.

(2) City Council may determine that the office of an elective city officer should be forfeited where the officer:

a. Based on a recommendation of the Board of Ethics to the City Council, is determined by a three-fourths vote of City Council Members serving to have violated the ethics ordinance or ethics provisions of the City Charter;

b. Refuses to cooperate in an investigation of the Inspector General, Board of Ethics or Ombudsperson;

c. Neglects or refuses to comply with the provisions of the City Charter after being provided an opportunity to comply pursuant to Section 7.5-209; or

d. Violates any provision of the City Charter punishable by forfeiture.

(Part 3, Sec. 5, Res. of 6-26-2018, eff. 10-15-2018)

