(a) Under state law as delineated in this section and as provided for in the Charter, the City is authorized to enact ordinances which provide for the following four types of penalties:

(1) Misdemeanors.

a. Where a provision of this Code is designated as a misdemeanor but a penalty is not provided therein, the penalty shall not exceed, upon conviction, a fine of $500.00 or imprisonment for 90 days, or both in the discretion of the court, provided, that where the violation substantially corresponds to a violation of state law that is a misdemeanor for which the maximum period of imprisonment is 93 days, the provision may provide that any person, upon conviction for the violation of any such provision of this Code, shall be punished by a fine of not more than $500.00, or by imprisonment not to exceed 93 days, or by both such fine and imprisonment in the discretion of the court, for each such offense. In addition, the City may adopt Section 625(1)(c) of the Michigan Vehicle Code, being MCL 257.625(1)(c), by reference in Chapter 46 of this Code, Traffic and Vehicles, and shall provide that a violation thereof is punishable by one or more of the following:

1. Community service for not more than 360 hours;

2. Imprisonment for not more than 180 days;

3. A fine of not less than $200.00 or more than $700.00;

b. Whenever in this Code, any act is prohibited or is made or declared to be unlawful or an offense, or whenever the doing of any act is required or the failure to do any act is declared to be unlawful, and the violation thereof is not designated as a misdemeanor, a civil infraction, a blight violation or a municipal civil infraction, and no specific penalty is provided therefor, in accordance with Section 4i(k) of The Michigan Home Rule City Act, being MCL 117.4i(k), a person shall be punished, upon conviction for the violation of any such provision of this Code, by a fine of not more than $500.00, or by imprisonment not to exceed 90 days, or by both such fine and imprisonment in the discretion of the court, for each such offense;

(2) Civil infractions. In accordance with Section 41(1) of the Michigan Home Rule City Act, being MCL 117.41(1), consistent with Sections 1 through 923 of the Michigan Vehicle Code, being MCL 257.1 through 257.923, and Sections 1 through 3 of the Michigan Control in Traffic in Parking Areas Act, being MCL 257.941 through 257.943, when enacting or amending Chapter 46 of this Code, Traffic and Vehicles, the City shall designate a violation of any provision, which is not designated as a misdemeanor under MCL 257.1 through 257.923, or MCL 257.941 through 257.943, as a civil infraction and provide a civil fine for violation thereof commensurate with MCL 257.1 through 257.923, or MCL 257.941 through 257.943;

(3) Blight violations. In accordance with Section 41(4) of The Michigan Home Rule City Act, being MCL 117.41(4), and as specifically authorized by this Code, the City may determine that a violation of this Code be decriminalized, cease to be a misdemeanor, and be designated a blight violation that is subject to the assessment of a civil monetary fine and is issued, served, and processed in accordance with Chapter 3, Article II, of this Code, Enforcement of Blight Violations; and

(4) Municipal civil infractions. Unless already designated by state statute as a municipal civil infraction, in accordance with Chapter 87 of the Michigan Revised Judicature Act of 1961, being MCL 600.8701 et seq., and Section 4l(2) and (3) of the Michigan Home Rule City Act, being MCL 117.4l(2) and (3), the City may determine that a violation of this Code be decriminalized, cease to be a misdemeanor, and be deemed a municipal civil infraction that is subject to the assessment of a civil monetary fine and is issued, served, and processed in accordance with Chapter 3, Article III, of this Code, Enforcement of Municipal Civil Infractions.

