In accordance with Section 8b(11) of the Stille-DeRossett-Hale Single State Construction Code Act, being MCL 125.1508b(11), the City adds to Section 102 of the 2015 Michigan Mechanical Code, Applicability, the following provisions, which were contained in the 1984 Detroit City Code prior to July 31, 2001, and concern administration and enforcement:

102.3.1 Frequency of mechanical inspections. The owner, or the owner’s designated agent, of a mechanical system are subject to action by the Code Official, and designated City inspectors, who are authorized, after providing notice:

(1) To inspect any system, which requires a permit to be installed or altered;

(2) To make biennial inspections of all oil-burning and gas-burning appliances, and equipment thereof, which are governed by this code, provided, that biennial inspections are not required:

(a) For such appliances, and equipment thereof, installed in single- or two-family residences; or

(b) For such appliances, and equipment thereof, used exclusively for residential purposes in other buildings, where the manufacturer’s rated hourly input does not exceed three gallons for each oil-burning appliance or 200,000 BTU for each gas-burning appliance;

(3) To make biennial inspections, in other than single- and two-family residences, of the following refrigerating systems:

(a) Self-contained refrigerating systems actuated by motors or engines having a standard rating of over two horsepower; and

(b) Refrigerating systems, other than the self-contained refrigerating systems, actuated by motors or engines having a standard rating of over one horsepower;

(4) To inspect all equipment and systems that are governed by this code, which, due to their condition, may be dangerous to life or property;

(5) To make, or cause to be made, such other inspections and tests as are deemed necessary for the purpose of safety and the enforcement of this code; and

(6) To issue orders for repair and correction of unsafe conditions and violation of this code.

102.3.2 Frequency of boiler and pressure vessel inspections. The owner, or the owner’s designated agent, of a boiler or a pressure vessel are subject to action by the Code Official, and designated City inspectors, who are authorized, after providing notice:

(1) To make an inspection of all boilers and pressure vessels which require permits under Chapter 10 of this code;

(2) To make annual internal and external inspections of all high-pressure boilers;

(3) To make annual inspections of all low-pressure boilers having a heating surface greater than 300 square feet with the inspections alternating between an internal inspection at one time and an external inspection at the other;

