In accordance with Section 8b(11) of the Stille-DeRossett-Hale Single State Construction Code Act, being MCL 125.1508b(11), the City adds to Section 109 of the 2015 Michigan Mechanical Code, Means of Appeal, the following provision, which was contained in the 1984 Detroit City Code prior to July 31, 2001, and concern administration and enforcement:

**109.1 Means of appeal.** An interested person, or the person’s authorized agent, aggrieved by a decision, determination, or order of the Code Official, also known as the Building Official, as defined in this code, which refuses to grant an application for a permit or makes any other decision pursuant or related to this code, may appeal in writing for a variance to the Board of Appeals in accordance with Chapter 8, Article I, of the 2019 Detroit City Code, Board of Appeals for Construction, and this section. An application for appeal shall be based on a claim that the true intent of the code or the rules governing construction have been incorrectly interpreted, the provisions of the code do not apply, or an equal or better form of construction is proposed. The decision of the Board of Appeals may be appealed to the Construction Code Commission in accordance with the act and the applicable time frames.

**Exception:** As permitted under Section 9c of the Stille-DeRossett-Hale Single State Construction Code Act, being MCL 125.1509c, the City enacts, through this provision, that a person is not eligible to apply for a variance where the person, or the owner of the affected or proposed building or structure, is delinquent in paying a civil fine, costs, or a justice system assessment imposed by the Blight Administrative Hearings Bureau established under Section 3-2-51 of the 2019 Detroit City Code, provided, that:

(1) This prohibition does not apply to an application for a permit where the work authorized under the permit will correct, in whole or in part, the blight violation that was the subject of the delinquent payment; and

(2) This prohibition does not apply to an applicant where the applicant became the owner of the property by foreclosure or by taking a deed in lieu of foreclosure and is one of the following:

a. 1. A government-sponsored enterprise as likewise defined in 2 USC 622(8) meaning a corporate entity created by a law of the United States that:

(i) Has a federal charter authorized by law;

(ii) Is privately owned, as evidenced by capital stock owned by private entities or individuals;

(iii) Is under the direction of a board of directors, a majority of which is elected by private owners;

