Subdivision D. Unsafe Equipment

In addition to the requirements of Section 3.18.3.8 of the ASME Code:

(1) Where hydraulic cylinders are buried underground, a protective coating acceptable to the Department shall be applied or a monitored cathodic protection system shall be installed that detects corrosion from galvanic or electrolytic action, salt water, or other underground conditions;

(2) No new hydraulic elevator installation shall incorporate buried piping except by specific permission from of the Department. Existing buried hydraulic piping may remain until replaced, at which time the replacement piping shall not be buried except with the specific permission of the Department;

(3) Prior to insertion of the hydraulic cylinder into the ground, the Department shall be notified that the hydraulic system is ready for inspection of the protective-coating or the monitoring cathodic protection system. It shall be unlawful to bury the hydraulic elevator components specified herein until the hydraulic system has been inspected as provided for by this section and accepted by the Department;

(4) An outer cylinder casing is required on a new hydraulic elevator or where a cylinder is being replaced. This casing shall be made from not less than schedule 30 PVC or steel pipe three-eighths of an inch in thickness. A glued cap or an expandable type concrete plug shall be poured into the bottom of a casing or a welded plate closer. A dry casing shall be provided. Where needed, a dry non-conductive material shall be provided between the cylinder and casing to secure position of the cylinder. When using a pressurized casing system, the system shall be air tight and maintain a pressure not to exceed 30 PSIG during inspection;

(5) Hydraulic elevators shall have all exposed horizontal runs of supply piping outside the elevator machine room enclosure protected by a substantial metal guard or shield of not less than one-eighth inch in thickness; and

(6) Oil line supports shall be provided to eliminate stress at joints and fittings.

(Code 1964, § 7.7; Code 1984, § 7.7; Ord. No. 307-H, § 1(7.7), eff. 3-2-1979; Ord. No. 02-87, § 1(7.7), eff. 2-20-1987; Ord. No. 03-97, § 1(7.7), eff. 3-10-1997)

(a) In addition to the requirements of the Section 5.2 of the ASME Code and this article, a limited-use/limited-application elevator shall comply with the following requirements:

(1) Only one limited-use/limited-application elevator per structure shall be allowed;

(2) The installation of a limited-use/limited-application elevator shall be limited to those structures that have a total square footage of not more than 20,000 square feet, except for one- or two-family dwellings, provided, that where the area to be accessed is 10,000 square feet or less, a limited-use/limited-application elevator may be installed in the structure;

(3) The minimum rated load of the limited-use/limited-application elevator shall conform to Section 2.16.1 of the ASME Code. The maximum rated load shall not exceed 635 kilograms (1,400 pounds);

(4) A limited-use/limited-application elevator shall not have a vertical rise of more than 25 feet; and

    a. Where the elevator has only a front opening, it shall serve only two floors; or

    b. Where the elevator has a front and rear opening and there is an intermediate level between two floors, three landings shall be permitted;

(5) The rated speed of a limited-use/limited-application elevator shall not be more than 30 feet per minute. Where the distance to be traveled by such a device is more than six feet, the minimum speed of the device shall not be less than 15 feet per minute;

(6) All exposed equipment on a limited-use/limited-application elevator shall be guarded to protect against accidental contact that may cause bodily injury;

(7) The frame of a limited-use/limited-application elevator shall be constructed of metal;

(8) The clear floor area of the car below the handrail shall be a minimum of 54 inches long, a minimum of 30 inches wide, and not be more than 13 square feet. The controls shall be located so as to not infringe upon the minimum 32-inch by 54-inch clear space below the handrail;

(9) All welding on the limited-use/limited-application elevator shall be in accordance with the ASME Code;

(10) The limited-use/limited-application elevator shall be fully enclosed on the top and on any side that is not used as an entrance or as an exit, and such enclosures shall conform to requirements of Section 2.14 of the ASME Code;

(11) Except where vision panels are provided, a limited-use/limited-application elevator door without openings shall be provided at each entrance to the elevator along with an electric contact device door or electric contact gate conforming to the requirements of Section 2.12.5 of the ASME Code. A door or gate handle shall be provided for manually-operated doors and shall be of the lever or bar type. The top of the handle shall not be more than 48 inches above the elevator floor;

(12) A handrail that extends the full length of the one long side of the platform shall be provided on the control side at a height of 36 inches to the top of the handrail above the finished floor of the limited-use/limited-application elevator. The bottom of the handrail shall be not less than 32 inches above the finished floor of the device;

(13) Except for direct-plunger hydraulic elevator, each limited-use/limited-application elevator shall be equipped with a safety mechanism and overspeed governor which, in the event the device descends at a rate of more than 150 percent of the rated speed, shall interrupt the electrical power supply to the brake and motor, grip the rails or by other means firmly bring the car loaded with the rated load to a stop within a traveling distance of 1.5 inches, and hold the elevator stationary. After use, the safety mechanism shall only be capable of release when the car is raised;

(14) The operating controls from any control station shall be of a momentary pressure automatic type. Push/pull-type and recessed controls on

