A lead hazard may be abated by a homeowner if all of the following conditions exist:

(1) The dwelling owner, or one or more of such owner’s family members, occupy the dwelling;

(2) The dwelling owner, or an adult family member, has completed a work safe practices training course approved by the Health Department;

(3) The lead hazard abatement is performed by the dwelling owner, or an adult family member, who has completed a work safe practices training course approved by the Health Department;

(4) Upon completion of the abatement of the lead hazard, a lead hazard clearance test is made by a person who is a certified lead hazard inspector/risk assessor or by a certified lead clearance technician, and the tests results reveal acceptable lead-dust levels under federal and state law or regulation;

(5) Results of the clearance test are reported by the inspector/risk assessor or clearance technician to the Michigan Lead Safe Housing Registry; and

(6) The abatement complies with the provisions of this article and the requirements of any lead hazard abatement order or violation notice issued by the Health Department’s approval letter.

