(a) Within 14 days of the date that the applicant is presented with a proposed repair-to-own contract, the applicant must negotiate any changes with the Buildings, Safety Engineering, and Environmental Department and execute the contract.

(b) The Buildings, Safety Engineering, and Environmental Department shall:

(1) Establish a timetable and plan for completing repairs to be stated in the repair-to-own contract, which in no event shall exceed 12 months;

(2) Advise the repair-to-own contractor of any available loan or grant programs;

(3) Monitor repair-to-own contract compliance and completion of required repairs;

(4) Respond to repair-to-own contractor requests to verify completion of repairs;

(5) Upon completion of the repair-to-own contract, deliver a notice of satisfaction of contract to the repair-to-own contractor; or

(6) In the case of a breach of the repair-to-own contract, declare a default, terminate the contract and refer it to the Law Department to pursue contract remedies, which include voiding the deed and quieting title in the circuit court. At least ten days prior to commencing any action under this subsection, the Law Department shall provide written notice of that action delivered by certified mail, return receipt requested, to the repair-to-own contractor.

(c) A breach may include, but is not limited to, the following:

(1) Material behavior or action which is contrary to the terms of the repair-to-own contract;

(2) Failure to commence work within 60 days of City Council approval of the repair-to-own contractual obligations under the terms of this article;

(3) Failure to perform any of the repair-to-own contractual obligations under the terms of this article.

(d) The Law Department shall prepare a contract which includes all of the obligations of the repair-to-own contractor and City, defines breaches and defaults, and indicates timeframes for curing any breaches or defaults.

(e) Upon acceptance of the agreement, a repair-to-own contractor shall adhere to the following provisions:

(1) Obtain and maintain liability insurance with the City listed as an additionally insured during the term of the contract;

(2) Hold the City harmless against all liabilities associated with repair and occupancy of the dwelling;

(3) Secure the dwelling against trespass and vandalism;

(4) Restore utilities and arrange for a pre-occupancy inspection;

(5) Obtain permits for required repairs;

(6) Make pre-occupancy repairs;

(7) Arrange for an occupancy inspection and secure an occupancy permit;

(8) Make repairs in accordance with contract timetable which shall in no event exceed 12 months;

(9) Obtain appropriate City inspections;

