(5) On a sidewalk, pedestrian walkway, or other pedestrian-oriented portion of a public place or public right-of way, the operator of an electric personal mobility device shall in all circumstances yield the right-of-way to pedestrians and shall undertake all measures necessary to avoid impact with or injury to pedestrians, interference with the flows of pedestrian traffic, or the creation of a nuisance or other threat to public health and safety. Examples of such measures include, but are not limited to, reducing the electric personal mobility device’s speed, directing the electric personal mobility device to maintain safe distance from pedestrians, and temporarily ceasing operation of, and dismounting from, the electric personal mobility device. The operator shall be solely responsible for any impact with or injury to pedestrians, interference with the flow of pedestrian traffic, or the creation of a nuisance or other threat to public health and safety involving the shared electric personal mobility device.

(b) The Director of the Department of Public Works may designate restrictions, in addition to those set forth in this article, to the hours and areas where electric personal mobility devices may be operated in a public place or in the public right-of way.

